riendly (crew cooperation), eats unhealthily (maybe health, not a criterion). Also not good at criticism (adaptability). So mixed.\n\nCandidate D strengths: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience), concentrates well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility). Concerns: moody, loner (crew cooperation). So strong technical and responsibility, but cooperation concerns.\n\nWe need to decide which candidate best fits overall. Candidate C seems strong on reliability and crew cooperation (positive atmosphere, concern for others). Communication difficulty is a concern but maybe can be mitigated. Adaptability is low. Candidate B also strong but communication tone issues. Which is more critical? For safety-critical, communication is vital. Difficulty communicating ideas could be a safety risk. Egocentric may affect crew dynamics. Candidate B's pretentiousness and wrong tone also affect crew. Both have issues.\n\nWe could ask for more info on Candidate D's interpersonal behavior (loner, moody) to see if that is a bigger issue. But we need to make a public contribution now. Perhaps we can suggest that while Candidate C's reliability is excellent, his communication difficulty may be a deal-breaker, and propose Candidate B as a better overall fit given his stress handling and technical competence, despite some communication tone issues that may be less severe. Or we could ask for clarification on Candidate D's crew cooperation.\n\nGiven we need to give a recommendation, we could shift vote to Candidate B, providing reasoning. Let's do that: argue that Candidate B's strengths across reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, and decision making (weather assessment) outweigh his communication tone issues, which may be trainable, whereas Candidate C's communication difficulty and lack of adaptability are more concerning for long-distance flights requiring continuous learning.\n\nThus we will state our current recommendation: Candidate B. Provide evidence: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills. Concern: pretentious, wrong tone. Explain why we think B is better than C. Also note we still need to discuss A and D.\n\nProceed."}
